  return { ok: false, error: "Marketing email cannot be sent yet: there is no unsubscribe link to include, and a marketing email without a functional unsubscribe is unlawful. Nothing was sent." };

That converts the accidental transport stub into a deliberate compliance guard that a B6 transport change cannot silently erase. Also correct the now-false comment at send.ts:43-44, and change tests/outbound.test.ts:286 to assert against the real send path (call sendMessage for an email and expect the refusal today; once the token route lands, expect /Unsubscribe: https/ in the sent body) so the test proves what its name claims. When the unsubscribe route is built, generate a per-contact token and pass `{ channel: "email", url }` — the union guarantees the footer carries it.

Revoke against the HANDSET, not one row id. In `recordOptOut` (src/app/api/inbound/sms/route.ts:165-201), replace the `limit(1)` lookup with a fan-out over every row holding the number plus its merged cluster, and write the revocation and `do_not_contact` to all of them in the one transaction:

```ts
// route.ts:3 — add inArray
import { eq, and, desc, inArray } from "drizzle-orm";

async function recordOptOut(
  tenantId: string,
  e164: string | null,
  content: string,
  occurredAt: Date,
): Promise<boolean> {
  if (!e164) return false;
  return withTenant(tenantId, async (tx) => {
    // An opt-out is about a handset, not a row id. The phone index is deliberately
    // not unique (contacts.ts:128) because duplicates sit in the merge queue while a
    // human decides, so EVERY row carrying this number must be revoked — otherwise
    // the unmerged sibling keeps `granted` and the next campaign texts her again.
    const holders = await tx
      .select({ id: contacts.id, mergedIntoId: contacts.mergedIntoId })
      .from(contacts)
      .where(eq(contacts.phoneE164, e164));
    if (holders.length === 0) return false;

    const ids = new Set<string>();
    for (const h of holders) {
      ids.add(h.id);
      if (h.mergedIntoId) ids.add(h.mergedIntoId); // survivors of merged holders
    }
    // ...and anything merged into those survivors, so the whole identity is covered.
    const children = await tx
      .select({ id: contacts.id })
      .from(contacts)
      .where(inArray(contacts.mergedIntoId, [...ids]));
    for (const c of children) ids.add(c.id);

    const all = [...ids];
    await tx.insert(consents).values(
      all.map((contactId) => ({
        tenantId,
        contactId,
        channel: "sms" as const,
        status: "revoked" as const,
        sourceForm: "SMS opt-out keyword",
        wordingShown: content.slice(0, 500),
        ipAddress: null,
        occurredAt,
      })),
    );

    await tx
      .update(contacts)
      .set({ doNotContact: true, updatedAt: new Date() })
      .where(inArray(contacts.id, all));

    return true;
  });
}
```

Notes on the patch:
- Add `id` itself (not just the survivor) to the set, so a merged loser also carries the revocation — consents are append-only and the gate reads the union, so covering both ends is free and keeps provenance on the row the message actually arrived against.
- `inArray` on an empty array is a Postgres error in Drizzle; the `holders.length === 0` early return guarantees `ids` is non-empty before either `inArray` call.
- RLS/`withTenant` already scopes the phone lookup to the tenant, matching the existing `contacts_tenant_phone_idx`.

Defence in depth (recommended, separate change): the write-time fan-out does not cover the reverse ordering — Jane texts STOP, then a later web form creates a fresh duplicate row via resolve.ts:106-127 with its own `granted` consent, which passes the gate again. Make `checkConsent` (src/server/outbound/gate.ts:80-85) widen the identity set to sibling rows sharing a non-null `phone_e164` for the sms channel (and honour their `do_not_contact`), so the gate refuses on handset-level evidence regardless of the order things happened in.

Also fix the same unordered `limit(1)` in `resolveByPhone` (route.ts:138-142) so the inbound STOP message itself is not filed against an arbitrary duplicate — at minimum add a deterministic `.orderBy(asc(contacts.createdAt))`.

Tests: extend tests/outbound.test.ts (the merged-record case at :153 is the neighbouring precedent) with a case that inserts two live, unmerged contacts on one `phone_e164`, both sms-granted, posts a STOP to the webhook, and asserts BOTH rows end with `do_not_contact = true` and a `revoked` latest consent, and that `checkConsent` refuses for both. Add an email-channel assertion on the second row too, since `do_not_contact` is channel-independent.

In src/app/api/inbound/sms/route.ts, replace the exists-probe with a latest-state probe that asks the same question the gate asks, and lift the do_not_contact write out of the conditional:

      if (item.contactId) {
